WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.383 --> 00:00:05.463
Put a grid of electrodes on the retina.

2
00:00:06.363 --> 00:00:07.363
Do you get a grid of dots?

3
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:09.913
The Argus II tried it.

4
00:00:10.493 --> 00:00:14.273
A camera drove 60 electrodes on the inner surface of the retina.

5
00:00:15.367 --> 00:00:20.327
But ganglion cell axons run across that inner surface, toward the optic disk.

6
00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:25.400
Predict. Axons from elsewhere run under one electrode.

7
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.780
What does the person see when it fires?

8
00:00:33.883 --> 00:00:39.103
Often not a dot. Four implant users drew elongated or distorted shapes.

9
00:00:40.116 --> 00:00:45.576
Each passing axon carries signals from a ganglion cell that reports a different part

10
00:00:45.576 --> 00:00:46.576
of the visual field.

11
00:00:47.616 --> 00:00:52.716
Recruit cells along a bundle, and a stretch of visual locations can be activated at

12
00:00:52.716 --> 00:00:54.496
once, which can produce a streak.

13
00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:58.650
A model of axon pathways accounted for the shapes.

14
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:02.410
It is a model-supported explanation, not a recording.

15
00:01:03.483 --> 00:01:07.083
The current reaches neurons whose place on the map is somewhere else.

16
00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:12.093
So move the electrode away from the axons, and keep each field small.

17
00:01:12.993 --> 00:01:14.133
The PRIMA chip does both.

18
00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:17.253
Near-infrared light powers its pixels.

19
00:01:18.213 --> 00:01:23.113
Each pixel stimulates bipolar cells, and a local return electrode confines its

20
00:01:23.113 --> 00:01:24.113
field.

21
00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:26.163
Acuity is scored in logMAR.

22
00:01:27.203 --> 00:01:30.643
0 is 20/20. 1.0 is 20/200.

23
00:01:31.843 --> 00:01:32.843
Higher is worse.

24
00:01:33.466 --> 00:01:37.626
Predict. Each pixel is 100 micrometres across.

25
00:01:38.586 --> 00:01:41.706
If its field stays within the pixel, what acuity could it give?

26
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:49.310
The conversion is on screen.

27
00:01:50.230 --> 00:01:54.790
The pixel limit is near 1.32 logMAR, about 20/420.

28
00:01:57.033 --> 00:02:03.473
In the PRIMAvera trial, mean acuity at the implant's own resolution was 1.32 logMAR.

29
00:02:04.433 --> 00:02:07.013
The measured mean sits near the pixel limit.

30
00:02:08.053 --> 00:02:11.653
Eyes differ in size, so treat it as agreement, not a precise prediction.

31
00:02:12.766 --> 00:02:17.126
For the Argus II, an ideal grid would give about 20/2400.

32
00:02:18.306 --> 00:02:22.866
Yet its best reported grating acuity was about 20/1260.

33
00:02:23.926 --> 00:02:26.046
So this is an estimate, not a hard limit.

34
00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:31.530
Two cautions. A zoomed score adds a magnifier, not pixels.

35
00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:36.090
And serious device- or procedure-related events came with the benefit.

36
00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.640
Optogenetics changes the cells instead.

37
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:44.180
Ganglion cells are made to express a light-gated channel, ChrimsonR.

38
00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:49.660
Goggles supply the bright amber light, and part of the encoding the retina would

39
00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:50.660
have done.

40
00:02:50.983 --> 00:02:54.923
With the goggles, one patient located, counted and touched objects.

41
00:02:55.763 --> 00:02:58.163
Without them, the patient did not detect objects.

42
00:02:59.216 --> 00:03:02.756
Where current reaches the wrong neurons, percepts can be distorted.

43
00:03:03.836 --> 00:03:07.836
PRIMA's mean acuity sits near its pixel limit, as its design aims for.

44
00:03:08.656 --> 00:03:12.236
With optogenetics, the goggles' light decides which cells are driven.
